<h2 id="manufacturers-heading">Cleaning System Suppliers</h2>
<p>Belt cleaners and cleaning systems are available from conveyor OEMs, component specialists, and cleaning equipment suppliers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Martin Engineering</strong> specializes in bulk conveyor belt cleaners, scrapers, and dust suppression systems.</li>
<li><strong>Flexco</strong> manufactures belt cleaners, tracking systems, and belt maintenance products.</li>
<li><strong>ASGCO</strong> (American Standard Global Company) provides belt cleaners, idlers, and conveyor products for mining and industrial applications.</li>
<li><strong>Dorner</strong> and <strong>mk North America</strong> offer washdown conveyor designs with sanitary cleaning features for food and pharmaceutical packaging.</li>
<li><strong>Spraying Systems Co.</strong> supplies spray nozzles and wash systems for CIP and manual cleaning applications.</li>
<li><strong>Conveyor component distributors</strong> (Motion Industries, Applied Industrial Technologies) stock replacement scraper blades and cleaning accessories.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a conveyor belt cleaning system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A conveyor belt cleaning system removes product residue, debris, and carryback from the belt surface to maintain sanitary conditions, prevent contamination, reduce belt wear, and keep return rollers clean. Belt cleaners include primary scrapers (mounted at the head pulley), secondary scrapers (mounted behind the primary), brush cleaners, air knives, and washdown systems. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and pharmaceutical applications</a> require FDA-compliant materials and designs that can be sanitized.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a clean-in-place (CIP) conveyor system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A clean-in-place (CIP) conveyor system uses fixed spray nozzles or spray bars mounted on the conveyor to apply rinse water, detergent, and sanitizer to the belt and frame without disassembly. CIP gives repeatable cleaning cycles, reduces labor, and can produce documented sanitation records. It only works on a conveyor designed for it: components rated for the spray pressure and temperature, materials compatible with the chemicals, and a frame that drains completely. See <a href="/conveyor-types/washdown-conveyors/">washdown conveyors</a> for component ratings.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between primary and secondary belt cleaners?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Primary belt cleaners mount at the head pulley discharge point and remove the bulk of carryback material from the belt. They use urethane, polyurethane, or tungsten carbide blades depending on the abrasiveness of the material. Secondary belt cleaners mount behind the primary on the return side of the belt and remove finer residue that the primary cleaner missed. Multiple secondary cleaners may be used in series for heavy carryback applications.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How often should conveyor belts be cleaned?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary conveyors</a> in food and pharmaceutical plants typically require cleaning after each production run, at shift changes, and whenever product changeover occurs. Distribution and manufacturing conveyors may only need weekly or monthly cleaning unless visible buildup occurs. Continuous belt cleaners (scrapers, brushes) operate during normal production to prevent buildup.</p>

</div>
</div>
